  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 126 PM EDT Friday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: A weak shortwave will pass through the region \nfrom northwest to southeast this afternoon and evening, but the \nstrongest dynamics will mostly be to the south. An associated \narea of showers and embedded thunderstorms has developed west of\nOttawa and those will move southeast into southwestern St. \nLawrence County. A briefly stronger storm is possible there. The\nstorms should mostly pass south of Rutland County, though a \nbrief isolated shower is possible there. Pea sized hail and \ngusty winds are possible in the strongest storm in St. Lawrence \nCounty. After that, a gorgeous opener to the weekend will occur \non Saturday with seasonably cool temperatures and low humidity.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: A double barrel low will move into the region \nlate Sunday into Sunday night, bringing rounds of rain. The \nprimary low will track well north of the St. Lawrence, while the\nsecondary low will try to spin up off the coast of southern New\nEngland, but they will both remain on the weaker side. The Euro\nensembles generally bring the heavier rain associated with the \nsouthern low farther north and into the region, while the GEFS \nmembers generally split the region between the areas of low \npressure and bring lighter precip. Overall, combined \nEPS/GEFS/CMCE probabilities of receiving more than a half inch \nare between 40-70 percent, and the probabilities of seeing more \nthan an inch are less than 10 percent. Some convective \nenhancement is possible across southern areas, but no flooding \nis expected. Very weak lapse rates, a notable inversion and \nunfavorable timing should prevent much of a severe threat, \nthough a trend north in storm track could still produce \nsomething, so the threat will continue to be watched. A few \nlingering showers are possible on Monday as a weak shortwave \nmoves through under broad cyclonic flow.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3: High pressure and calm weather embedded in broad\ncyclonic flow will continue through Tuesday as temperatures \nslowly rise to near or just above seasonable levels. Highs will \nbe in the upper 70s to low 80s heading into the mid week. \nHumidity will remain on the lower side Tuesday, but as flow \nshift slightly to the southwest by late Tuesday into Wednesday, \nsome moisture advection is expected by mid week. The next \nweather maker will arrive by Wednesday into Thursday with a cold\nfrontal passage. Models are in pretty good agreement for rain \nand possible thunder chances Wednesday afternoon for the higher \nterrain and into late Wednesday for most locations, however, how\nlong showers linger remains in question. The GFS is the fastest\nof the model suite clearing the area of precipitation by \nThursday afternoon. The Euro and Canadian favor a longer system,\nwith more positive tilting to the trough axis, and \nprecipitation persisting through the day Thursday. Regardless, \npwats look to increase to around an inch, though where the best \nforcing sets up will determine how much of the precipitable \nwater is realized. Temperatures mid to late week will slowly \ndecrease from the low 80s to the upper 70s, along with a drying \nsurface with dewpoints sinking back down to the low 50s.",
